 and even  defense attorneys become bureaucrats  after a while there's sort of going  through the motions or not looking at  the evidence objectively anymore they  assume everybody's guilty and so they're  not sitting there you know thinking  hahahahaha I'm gonna convict an innocent  person they just become close-minded and  they become so biased in their  evaluation of the cases that they're no  longer looking at them objectively and  fairly and I talk about how I did that  as a prosecutor  it's tunnel vision it's confirmation  bias there's a whole chapter on how  confirmation bias affects the judgments  of police and prosecutors even forensic  experts you know the system basically  what it comes down to is our criminal  justice system right now is operating on  an understanding of the human mind  understanding of psychology from the  1800s or at best the 1950s and our  understanding of the human mind has  changed so much but the criminal justice  system hasn't caught up so for example  wrongful convictions are caused many of  them by human memory problems you know  eyewitnesses are so easily open to  suggestion and you can as a prosecutor  and a police officer you can bend their  statements to fit your theories of the  case but we tend to in the court system  think that eyewitnesses come and testify  and say I'm a hundred percent positive  that's the guy did it  but that's ironclad evidence it's  actually really weak evidence because  you know human memory is very very weak  and particularly the way we handle it in  the criminal justice system we  contaminate it and what be an example of  the opposite I'm interested because  obviously the idea that someone comes in  and says I'm a hundred percent sure this  is how it happened we know that that's  that that's that's not really the case  but can you give us an example of how a  prosecutor might take a witness that  does not seem to have 100% recollection  and  use the power of suggestion to create a  particular impression upon the jury oh  yeah I knew that I talk about this all  the time I mean I did it as a prosecutor  I saw it done around me all the time  I'll give you a perfect example I get  many of them in the book you know you  got a case where you think the murder  happened at 11 o'clock and you know that  the the guy who's your suspect was in  the bar that evening and so you pull a  couple witnesses in and the first couple  people who were in the bar that day say  yeah I think he left around 10:30 and he  came back around 11:30 and so as a  prosecutor you're going bingo right  that's perfect that's he was gone right  when the murder happened he had time to  get there to commit the murder and get  back you call the third witness in and  the third witness goes you know what he  was there till midnight well what does  that mean for you as a police officer a  prosecutor that's that's an alibi that  means the guy couldn't have done it and  so in the mine frame you're in as a  police officer prosecutor you just  assume this person has to be wrong and  so you start grilling them I mean are  you sure I think really hard because you  know other people are telling us bah  blah blah blah blah and it doesn't take  that off that much to get some witnesses  and many witness will start bending and  they'll start saying you know the more I  think about it you're probably you know  I think he had left by the time the  football game was over and it starts to  line up now with the time frame that the  prosecution had all along now just a  procedural question is it allowed to  reference other testimony to someone  you're questioning in that way is it  okay to say well other witnesses have  said this other thing you're not you're  not supposed to there's no official rule  against it I mean the defense attorney  can get on the stand and say you know  did they suggest to you what to say did  they tell you what other witnesses are  saying right and some witnesses might  say yeah they said that but it didn't  affect my you know it didn't affect me  or they may say no they told me that you  know but you know they can be done more  subtly you know you can if you're after  you're a prosecutor police officer and  you're asking those pointed questions  over and over again you don't even have  to say that's what other witnesses are  telling us you know you can be looking  each other that you know the prosecutor  looks at the police officer and they  stare at each other and they just look  shake their heads and dis plead the  witness gets the message after a while  that they're wrong mm-hm and that they  should be really thinking about it and  then you come back to me say look I want  you to think really hard about this  that's not jiving up with what we're  hearing you know this happens and what I  talk about in the book that is even more  alarming is in my prosecutor's office  when a witness changed their statement  to  our prodding it was often the case that  we didn't write it down we didn't write  down what their first statement it was  so that the original defense attorney at  trial never finds out that there was an  evolution so doesn't get a chance to  cross-examine them and say hey wait a  minute didn't you first say that he was  there until midnight that means he  couldn't expended the murder and when I  first started I was told you know don't  write that down because you got a really  Prada witness on their memory and to  help them understand and help them  remember and help them understand how  serious this is to tell the truth and  once you get them you know once they  come to Jesus which means give the right  answers they're supposed to give her you  want them to give then you write it down  but anything before that is just an  offhand comment it's not official you  know you really have to prod their  memory first and you know at the time I  didn't understand how malleable human  memory was so I sort of bought into the  theory like you know we really know what  happened we've got the whole file this  guy if he says something that disagrees  with what we think he's dead wrong and  so it's our job to correct him now that  I understand human memory better  I understand how we were doing what's  called a call the book synthesized  testimony we are creating testimony to  fit our theory of the case we're  speaking with Mark Godsey who's  co-founder of the Ohio Innocence Project  the book is blind injustice a former  prosecutor exposes the psychology and  politics of wrongful convictions we'll  pause on our conversation with him and  continue with more tomorrow we're  continuing our conversation with Mark  Godsey who's the co-founder of the Ohio  Innocence Project which has freed 25  wrongly convicted people who served the  combined 470 years in prison he's also  author of blind injustice a former  prosecutor exposes the psychology and  politics of wrongful convictions before  we move on to how you work to free  people of mark one other thing I want to  touch on is how much of a factor do you  believe it is in terms of wrongful  convictions that a lot of a lot of  people just don't have the money for  better defense attorneys and they may  not be receiving the best defense  particularly from overloaded public  defenders who might have 50 or even  several hundred cases at a time yeah I  talked about that in detail in chapter 3  of the book there's a great imbalance in  the system you know when I was a  prosecutor if I needed a fancy CSI  expert to come and spend the evidence a  particular way any kind of expert or  Bell and whistle I needed to get  conviction I had as a defendant you know  unless you're really rich I mean the  public sometimes see these defenses from  like Michael Jackson or OJ Simpson and  they think that's what everybody gets  and you know not at all public defenders  are overworked I think in Louisiana one  of them said that they get an average of  seven minutes they can spend on each  case you know it varies from  jurisdiction to jurisdiction but they  don't have the funds to hire  investigators to go out and beat the  streets and find witnesses they don't  have the funds for all these bells and  whistles and fancy experts and all this  kind of stuff I mean in some cases they  can ask and they can get some funds but  as a defense attorney you've got to pick  and choose because if you're asking too  much the judges are gonna get tired of  you so the average defendant is really  out of resources disadvantage one of the  reasons why I love the documentary  making a murderer and I hope that  everybody who's interested in this issue  watches it is that you can see that  disparity play out in great detail  there's two defendants in the case that  are charged in Steven Avery has a lot of  money because he just won a civil case  and so he hires Dean Strang and and  Jerry blanking on his name but you know  they do a fantastic job I mean they  can't get better defense attorneys than  that and then the other defendant  Brendan Dassey gets a public defender  and one after the other who just you  know sells him down the river basically  admits in court that he's guilty before  even interviews as his own client and so  you can see that disparity now I don't  mean to disparage public defenders I  mean they're doing a thankless job and  they're overworked and underpaid and  they have big hearts and all these sorts  of things but a lot of places you get  some of them that's become burned-out  and they're going through the motions  and you know if it's your child who's  charged with this crime  you're gonna want somebody who's who's  not doing that who's invested and has  the resources but you know that's rare  so when you get involved first of all I  mean how do you even pick which cases to  work on right how do you first determine  before you start actually making the  case to the system that someone may have  been wrongfully convicted you have to  sort of research the case for yourself  so what's that process like for you to  decide what cases to take on yeah it's a  very fluid process so since we started  in 2003 we've had over 8,000 Ohio  inmates write to us we investigate a  pretty high percentage I'd say 50% of  all off the top you can cut kind of  early because it's clear they're not  claiming innocence they're saying I did  it but I  too long of a sentence or you know  things like that they're sort of  admitting it we're only looking for  people that are totally innocent and so  the other 50% sort of go on a conveyor  belt and they start falling off and  eventually after you can just keep going  for a while you have these cases that  are still on the conveyor belt you know  they still look good after that you  continue to investigate and investigate  so the first thing we do is if it seems  to meet our criteria the persons  claiming they're innocent you know we  just investigate as much as we can about  the case from the paper what you can  find on the internet newspaper articles  the decisions from the case from the  court we will do public records requests  and get the police files in the  prosecutors file so we're looking for  was there DNA collected at the time by  the police that hasn't been tested that  can tell us one way or another that's an  easy low-hanging fruit you know if you  got a guy saying he's innocent who's  convicted of rape and there's a rape kit  there that had semen collected it was  never tested but we can get an answer  right now you know fairly quickly  whether he's innocent or guilty in other  cases it's much more complicated there's  no DNA that can get sort of give us a  quick answer you know it involves going  back and talking to the witnesses from  20 years ago when the person was  convicted and you know a lot of times  you do that and everything is the same  they sort of say yeah I saw what I saw  on the guy's guilty and but you know  with a lot of these people claiming  innocence you start looking into it and  it starts to smell a little bit you  start hearing you know I lied for this  reason or that you know the police are  pressuring me they were sort of  manipulating my testimony or you know  you do a public records request and you  get the police file and you find all  this exculpatory evidence all this stuff  that points to the defendants innocence  that the police never turned over which  they're required to under the  constitutional Brady doctrine so we want  to think last six of our cases in a row  then because the police  suppress hid evidence that pointed to  innocence back at the time of trial so  there's all sorts of different angles I  mean we're just basically rolling up our  sleeves like old fashioned detectives  and looking into these cases and seeing  if they still hold water after 20 30 40  years and legally speaking a court can  decide whether or not they will allow  you to even go through the process of  challenging an old conviction right I  mean there's no guarantee that you you  would even get that opportunity is that  right well we can file what's called a  post-conviction motion motion for new  trial which is a brief that'll have all  the evidence attached and half a day  for more the DNA test reports and it's  up to the court to decide whether it  meets the legal standards to reopen the  case and I gotta tell you there's a lot  of variance I mean a system I mean  chapter 2 of my book is called blind  denial and it's about how the system is  basically in denial about this problem  everybody sort of smugly believes that  wrongful convictions don't happen even  after we've been proving they happen you  know many judges and prosecutors no  matter what the evidence they're not  going to admit the mistakes been made  and so at the first step it's sort of a  real crapshoot do you have a judge who's  been enlightened to this problem and can  actually look at the evidence in an open  mind and many times we have overwhelming  evidence of 