t they remember that well  yeah so it's usually somebody who's  mentally ill that tries to claim that  they committed a crime they did and that  doesn't happen very often other than  that there's two major categories one is  where the person is coerced  I'm just intensive interrogation for  hours and hours and hours and here's a  dirty little secret the public doesn't  generally know police officers are  allowed to and do lie to suspects in the  interrogation room they'll do these  little stunts where the phone will ring  in the interrogation room and the  officer will answer it go huh that's  very interesting we just got the DNA  reports and your DNA was found on the  bloody knife now you can't even run DNA  test that quickly but they lie and then  the person sitting there you know  they're exhausted this has been going on  forever they start to and then the  officer starts to say you know your only  opportunity really to avoid the death  penalty is to tell me now I hope you  I'll get leniency all we want to do is  figure out what happened and we know  that you were provoked and we know this  was probably self-defense and so they  get them thinking I I have no choice I'm  going to get convicted and a rational  person starts to decide the lesser evil  is this is just to go along with what  the police said and I know everybody  says that I would never falsely confess  to a crime I didn't commit  but it happens and I if you if you're  saying that now I would encourage you to  read John Grisham's the innocent man  it's his only non-fiction book and  there's about a twenty page passage in  there when he takes you through a real  case why someone falsely confessed and I  think if anybody reads that they can  start to understand it the other major  category is after all this being told  that your you know your your DNA is at  the crime scene we found your  fingerprints and then the police  officers start saying you know you  probably blacked out I mean this happens  all the time they'll start saying I  blacked out before and I don't remember  and you get the person's start  questioning their memory and this goes  back to the valuable memory problem  where the person will start saying you  know what I I must have done it then and  I just don't remember then the officer  will help them remember and then after  three or four hours later when they've  had the officer tell them how the crime  happened so many times and they've  pictured in their mind while the officer  was telling them they started confused  what's real memories versus what they  just pictured earlier and and many  people who have been exonerated who gave  these false confessions describe that  process where you know they just  confused me and I started to question my  own memory and I didn't think cops could  lie and I thought well god I must have  done it and then and then so I I just I  just came up with these memories but I  know they're not there now false and you  know if somebody's listening to this and  they're confused by this I encourage you  to study the problem of malleable  memories I talked about it in my book  there's a whole chapter dedicated to it  but an easier way is just Google  Elizabeth Loftus or TED talk at 17  minutes long it takes you through this  and explains very easily how that can  happen the book is fascinating it's  called blind and justice a former  prosecutor exposes the psychology and  politics of wrongful convictions we've  been speaking with the book's author  mark Godsey whose co-founder of the  aiyoh innocence project mark thanks so  much for talking to us about this thank  you appreciate you have me on
 

